<p>A method for sequencing organisational change.</p>

<p>Most change programmes fail not because they choose the wrong interventions, but because they sequence them badly or not at all. The typical programme plan presents a hierarchy of workstreams running in parallel, connected by a Gantt chart that nobody reads and a governance structure that creates the illusion of coordination without establishing causal logic.</p>

<p>The Beat Network borrows a structural discipline from screenwriting - the beat sheet - and extends it into a networked model for organisational change. Where a screenplay has one audience following one sequence, an organisation has multiple groups experiencing change concurrently from different starting points. The Beat Network preserves the rigour of the beat sheet (flat, sequential, no hiding in hierarchy) while adding the cross-cutting dependencies, ground-truth conditions, and observable signals that organisational change demands.</p>

<p>A Beat Network is a set of concurrent, numbered sequences (changes), each describing a coherent line of organisational change. Each set of changes is a flat list of beats - complete sentences, one job per beat, each earning the next. Sets of changes are connected at explicit synchronisation points (gates) where progress in one set depends on progress in another. Every beat is grounded in an observable condition and validated by an observable signal.</p>

<p>The playbook is structured in seven parts:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/primitives/">Primitives</a></strong> - the six elements of the method and how they relate.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/writing-beats/">Writing Beats</a></strong> - rules for writing beats, changes, and connections.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/conditions-and-stalls/">Conditions and Stalls</a></strong> - working with ground truth and what happens when signals do not arrive.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/mmo-principle/">The MMO Principle</a></strong> - the structural metaphor and what the method is not.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/governance/">Governance</a></strong> - two roles, one interface, and how the network is held.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/worked-example/">Worked Example</a></strong> - a sketch of three sets of changes for a product operating model adoption.</li>
  <li><strong><a href="/playbooks/beat-network/how-to-use-it/">How to Use It</a></strong> - the sequence for building and running a Beat Network.</li>
</ol>

<p>The method is new. It will develop through use. This document is itself a beat sheet for the method: each section earns the next, and the test is whether the argument lands.</p>
